%llguidance {}

# Gemma 4 chat grammar (Lark / llguidance)
#
# Two named entries:
#   - `start` (default) — assistant-turn continuation; sampler entry. The model
#     emits an optional `<|channel>thought ...<channel|>` reasoning block, then
#     content, then either tool calls (`<|tool_call>call:NAME{args}<tool_call|>`)
#     or a `\`\`\`json` response_format block.
#   - `conversation` — full conversation wire shape; selected via arena
#     set_root() by the bidirectional walker.
#
# Conversation wire shape (per google-gemma-4-31B-it.jinja):
#   <|turn>system\n<system_body>[<|tool>...<tool|>]+<turn|>\n   (system + tool defs)
#   <|turn>user\n<user_body><turn|>\n
#   <|turn>model\n<assistant_body>[<|tool_call>...<tool_call|>]<turn|>\n
#                                  ^-- assistant body may contain <|channel>thought
#                                      <|tool_response>response:NAME{...}<tool_response|>
#   <|turn>model\n[<|channel>thought\n<channel|>]                     (generation prompt)
#
# {{RESPONSE_SCHEMA}} replaced at runtime with %json {schema}.

# --- Tag vocabulary -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every marker in this format is a tag: <|NAME> opens, <NAME|> closes,
# <|NAME|> is self-delimiting. Naming them here, once, is what stops the rest of
# the grammar from being a soup of literals in which "<|channel>thought" looks
# like one atom rather than a tag plus the channel's kind.
#
# Structure that follows from it:
#   <|channel>KIND ... <channel|>          a channel, with a kind
#   <|tool_call>DIRECTIVE:PAYLOAD<tool_call|>
#   <|tool>DIRECTIVE:PAYLOAD<tool|>
#   <|tool_response>DIRECTIVE:PAYLOAD<tool_response|>
#
# The three tool-family tags share one shape -- open, directive, colon, payload,
# close -- which the old literals hid completely.

# Each tag is a SPECIAL TOKEN -- llguidance's `<something>` syntax -- because that
# is what it is: one entry in Gemma 4's vocabulary. Checked against the model's
# own tokenizer:
#
#   <|turn>  105   <turn|>  106     <|tool>          46   <tool|>          47
#   <|channel> 100 <channel|> 101   <|tool_call>     48   <tool_call|>     49
#   <|think|>   98 <|"|>       52   <|tool_response> 50   <tool_response|> 51
#
# These were previously built from delimiter + name + delimiter
# (`TAG_L "channel" TAG_GT`) to keep `<|` and `|>` meaningful in their own right.
# That described the format correctly and the TOKEN STREAM wrongly: the model
# cannot emit `<|` alone, so there was nothing there for a grammar to reason
# about. The shape still holds -- `<|NAME>` opens, `<NAME|>` closes, `<|NAME|>` is
# self-delimiting -- it simply is not something the sampler can see inside of.
#
# Atomicity is also what makes every text body below a plain regex: a regex cannot
# produce a special token, so a body is bounded by whichever marker follows it,
# with no exclusion to hand-build and keep in step with this list.
turn_open_tag:            <|turn>
turn_close_tag:           <turn|>
channel_open_tag:         <|channel>
channel_close_tag:        <channel|>
tool_call_open_tag:       <|tool_call>
tool_call_close_tag:      <tool_call|>
tool_response_open_tag:   <|tool_response>
tool_response_close_tag:  <tool_response|>
tool_decl_open_tag:       <|tool>
tool_decl_close_tag:      <tool|>

# The self-delimiting form. The string delimiter's name is `"`, so it is not a
# bespoke marker; being symmetric is why the format cannot escape it -- see
# ARCHITECTURE.md. The think marker announces that the model may use its thought
# channel this turn.
str_delim_tag:  <|"|>
think_tag:      <|think|>

# KNOWN CONFORMANCE GAP -- multimodal placeholders are UNMODELLED.
#
# Google's official control-token list (ai.google.dev, Gemma 4 prompt formatting)
# names `<|image|>` and `<|audio|>` as "special placeholder tokens", and
# common_chat_gemma4_render emits `<|image|>`, `<|audio|>` AND `<|video|>` for
# typed content parts. Nothing here matches them.
#
# Consequence is one-sided but real: generation is unaffected (the model is never
# asked to emit a placeholder), while the `conversation` validator would REJECT
# any prompt carrying one, because a placeholder is a special token and the text
# bodies are regexes, which cannot produce one. Latent today only because this
# deployment runs with `no-mmproj = true` on both Gemma sections, so no request
# carries media.
#
# Fixing it means giving user/model turn bodies an alternation of text and
# placeholder rather than a single text run -- the same shape `model_body`
# already has for channels. Left undone deliberately rather than half-added.
# `<|video|>` needs checking first: the renderer emits it but the official token
# list does not mention it, so one of the two is wrong.

# Plain text bodies are LAZY LEXEMES, not tag-exclusion regexes.
#
# `foo[lazy]: /(.|\n)*/` tells llguidance to match as few bytes as possible, so
# the body stops as soon as whatever FOLLOWS it in the grammar can start. The
# structure decides where text ends -- which is why no text rule below mentions
# `<|`, `|>` or any tag name at all.
#
# This replaced a regex that tried to exclude tags itself. Two things were wrong
# with that, both measured against llguidance:
#
#   * Negative lookahead does not compile: "look-around, including look-ahead and
#     look-behind, is not supported". A grammar that fails to compile fails OPEN,
#     so the sampler ran with NO constraint and nothing said so.
#   * Rewriting it lookahead-free did not help either. llguidance lexes a regex
#     as one greedy lexeme with no backtracking, so a body able to consume even a
#     PREFIX of the following tag commits to it and then dead-ends.
#
# `lazy` is llguidance's own answer to this (docs/syntax.md, "Lazy lexemes"), and
# it is strictly better than any exclusion: text may contain `<`, `<html>` and
# backticks freely, because it is the grammar that ends the body, not a character
# class guessing which bytes might have been a tag.

# --- Entry rules -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This file defines productions; it deliberately has NO `start` rule. The entry
# is chosen per request from the state the prompt left the model in, and appended
# as `start: <rule>` by common_chat_grammar_set_entry -- llguidance compiles the
# rule literally named "start" and cannot be told to use another.
#
# The selectable entries, mapped from FSM states by gemma4_entry_roots:
#   turn_start        -- a fresh model turn; the model may open with a thought
#   resume_reasoning  -- generation resumes inside a thought already opened
#   conversation      -- a whole rendered prompt (the input validator)
#
# This grammar describes VALID Gemma 4 output and nothing else. It is the same
# grammar llguidance enforces during sampling, so a malformed construct is not
# something the model can emit -- and a production that tolerates one would be
# describing a language the format does not have. A stray `<|channel>` with no
# kind, or a `<channel|>` closing nothing, are parse errors here by design.
turn_start: channel_block? turn_tail

# Everything a model turn can contain after its opening thought. Shared by both
# generation entries so they cannot drift: `turn_start` and `resume_reasoning` differ
# ONLY in where the model picks up, never in what it may say next.
#
# ONE thought channel per GENERATED turn, which is what the documented ordering
# has: `<|turn>model -> <|channel>thought -> <channel|> -> ... -> <turn|>`. The
# opener is `turn_start`'s `channel_block?`; sampling may not open another.
#
# `{{EXTRA_CHANNELS}}` is how that differs between the two directions. It is
# empty for SAMPLING and `(channel_block content)*` for EXTRACTION, because those
# answer different questions -- what may the model emit, versus what might it
# have emitted. Re-imposing the sampler's bound on the parser could only reject
# output that was legitimately produced, which is the same reason extraction gets
# the permissive entry root and no tool schema.
#
# It used to be that repetition on BOTH sides, and on the sampling side it was an
# unbounded loop. `content` matches empty, so `<|channel>thought<channel|>` with
# an empty body was legal, and so was doing it again, forever. Measured live on
# the 12B the moment the tool branch stopped being an available escape hatch --
# asked to call a function that had not been declared, with nothing else the
# grammar would accept:
#
#   <|channel>thought.<channel|><|channel>thought.<channel|><|channel>thought...
#   finish_reason: length
#
# The same shape as the three runaways before it: something unbounded sitting
# where the turn needed to make progress. A second thought mid-turn is not part
# of the single-pass ordering anyway -- the post-tool-response re-opener begins a
# NEW generation pass, entered at `resume_reasoning` with the opener already in
# the prompt. `model_body` keeps the repetition unconditionally for the same
# reason the parse side does: a REPLAYED turn really can hold several channels,
# separated by tool responses.
#
# NOTE, not fixed here: entry_state IN_CONTENT shares `turn_start`, so after the
# thinking-off prefill (which puts an opened-and-closed thought in the PROMPT)
# the model may still open one more via `channel_block?`. Bounded -- one extra
# block, then no further repetition -- so it is not the runaway. Separating it
# needs its own entry rule per demand and its own conformance cases.
turn_tail: content {{EXTRA_CHANNELS}} ({{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} response_format turn_close_tag? | turn_close_tag?)

# `{{TOOL_CALL_ALT}}` is the tool-call alternative, and it is a PLACEHOLDER
# because whether a turn may call a tool is a property of the REQUEST, not of the
# format. It becomes `tool_call_request |` when the request declared tools with
# tool_choice other than "none", and NOTHING when it did not -- so a turn that was
# offered no tools cannot open a call at all.
#
# It was unconditional, and that was a hole rather than a nicety. `{{TOOL_SCHEMA}}`
# falls back to `tool_call_directive ":" func_name gemma4_dict?` with no tools
# declared -- any name, any arguments -- so a PLAIN CHAT REQUEST could emit a
# fabricated call to a function that was never offered. Measured by the mask walk
# on a request with no tools:
#
#   after ""   prose=OPEN   markers: <turn|> <|channel> <|tool_call>
#
# `<|tool_call>` had no business being in that set. Now it is absent, and the
# unconstrained fallback is reachable only from `conversation` -- the validator's
# entry, where a call the model already made has to be parseable whatever it
# named.
#
# The trailing `|` lives INSIDE the placeholder on purpose: substituting empty
# has to leave `(response_format ... | turn_close_tag?)`, not
# `( | response_format ...)`, which would add an empty alternative and quietly
# make the whole group optional.

# `<turn|>` is how the model CLOSES ITS OWN TURN, and leaving it out of the
# generation entries was a real, measured failure -- not a theoretical gap.
#
# Google lists it in eos_token_id ([1, 106, 50] = <eos>, <turn|>, <|tool_response>),
# so it is one of the three ways a Gemma 4 turn can legitimately end, and which
# one the model reaches for depends on context: after a tool response it wants
# `<turn|>`. Traced live on the 12B, answering a question after a tool result:
#
#   llguidance: step 16 | check token 106 -> REJECTED, re-sampling under mask
#   llguidance: step 16 | mask allows 262046 of 262144 tokens
#   next token: 9792 ' sugar'
#
# The model had finished ("There are 40,213 rows in the memory table.") and tried
# to close the turn. The grammar forbade the only token that ends it, `content`
# is unbounded so 262046 tokens were still legal, and the generation ran away
# into `sugar_bowl_01_10_2025_2025_01_01...` until max_tokens.
#
# It is optional rather than required because EOS also ends a turn and the model
# uses it too -- the same request with thinking off stopped cleanly on EOS. A
# tool-calling turn is the exception: it hands over at `<|tool_response>` and
# never reaches here, which is why the closer sits on the other two branches.

# The `tool_choice: "required"` pair of the two entries above. The model may
# still think first -- that is where it decides WHICH call to make -- but the
# turn then goes straight to the call. There is no `content` and no alternative,
# so a turn that does not call is unrepresentable.
#
# Enforcing it in the grammar is what makes it a guarantee rather than a request;
# instructing the model to "always call a tool" is the same unconstrained-and-
# usually-fine the arguments themselves were just moved out of.
#
# Content is deliberately NOT allowed before the call, and the first attempt that
# allowed it shows why. `content` is an unbounded regex, so
# `content* tool_call_request` says "you must call eventually" while never
# requiring the model to stop talking: asked something chatty with
# tool_choice=required, it answered in prose and then degenerated into repeated
# emoji until max_tokens, because EOS was masked (the call was still owed) and
# more content was always legal. A required call has to be reachable in bounded
# output, which means it has to be the ONLY thing that follows.
turn_start_tool_call: channel_block? tool_call_request

# The `response_format` pair. Same shape and the same reasoning as the
# tool_choice pair above: a caller that asked for a schema gets a turn that
# CANNOT be satisfied by prose.
#
# It was an alternative in `turn_tail`, which meant "the model may answer in the
# schema", not "must". Measured on the 12B with a two-property schema and
# additionalProperties:false, it answered with three properties of its own
# choosing and the request was reported as a success -- the schema had been
# compiled into the grammar and then simply not taken.
#
# This matters more than the tool case for the workflow that drives this fork:
# its Architect, Validator and cache stages are all schema-constrained calls
# whose output is parsed, not read.
# No `content` before the block, for the third time and the same reason. The
# first version of this rule allowed the model to narrate first, on the theory
# that the fenced opener would bound the text. It does not: `content` is an
# unbounded regex, so "narrate, then emit the block" permits narrating forever.
# Traced live -- 512 steps, every one `check token -> ok`, the mask never
# rejecting anything, while the model wrote its JSON as prose and never reached
# the block it still owed:
#
#   llguidance: step 511 | check token 236770 -> ok
#   n_decoded = 512, n_remaining = 0
#
# Unbounded content next to a required continuation is the recurring shape of
# this bug -- it produced the tool_choice=required emoji runaway and the missing
# <turn|> runaway too. A required thing must be the ONLY thing that follows.
#
# The tool-call alternative rides alongside, on the same `{{TOOL_CALL_ALT}}`
# terms as everywhere else: with tools declared the turn may call one INSTEAD of
# emitting the block, and with none it may not. That does not weaken the demand
# -- both branches are bounded, and neither is `content` -- it just means "answer
# in this schema" and "use a tool" are not mutually exclusive requests.
turn_start_response_format: channel_block? ({{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} response_format turn_close_tag?)
resume_reasoning_response_format: reasoning channel_close_tag ({{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} response_format turn_close_tag?)

# The caller's OWN grammar, as `response_format: {"type": "lark_grammar"}` or
# `{"type": "gbnf_grammar"}` (GBNF is converted to Lark first -- llguidance reads
# only Lark and JSON Schema).
#
# It is a SUBGRAMMAR, referenced through `{{USER_GRAMMAR}}`, not text pasted into
# this file. llguidance composes a list of grammars and lets one reference
# another by name (`@response_grammar`, docs/syntax.md "Multiple grammars"), each
# with its own lexeme class -- so a caller rule called `content` or `start`
# cannot collide with the ones here, and nothing has to rename anything.
#
# Deliberately ONE sampler. The alternative -- and what the top-level `grammar`
# extension used to do -- is to run the caller's grammar as a second sampler
# beside this one. Two grammars masking one token stream intersect their
# languages, and if neither is a superset the intersection can be EMPTY: every
# logit -INF mid-generation, with no diagnostic. As a production inside this
# grammar there is one language and one mask, which is also why `tools` and
# `response_format` can now be sent together.
#
# Unlike `response_format`, the caller's grammar is NOT fenced: it describes the
# answer text itself, so it stands where content would. Same discipline as the
# two entries above -- no `content` beside it, or "must" degrades to "may".
#
# And the same `{{TOOL_CALL_ALT}}`, which is what makes "either call a tool or
# answer in this grammar" expressible. It is the shape an agent stage actually
# wants: here are your tools, and if you are not going to use one, the answer
# looks like THIS. Both branches are bounded, so neither weakens the other -- the
# rule that a required thing must be the only thing that follows is about
# unbounded `content`, not about having two definite alternatives.
#
# This needed the placeholder to exist first. Offered unconditionally, a request
# with no tools would have got the unconstrained `{{TOOL_SCHEMA}}` fallback as an
# escape hatch out of the caller's own grammar -- strictly worse than not
# offering it, which is why it was left out until the branch could be made
# conditional.
turn_start_user_grammar: channel_block? ({{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} user_content turn_close_tag?)
resume_reasoning_user_grammar: reasoning channel_close_tag ({{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} user_content turn_close_tag?)
user_content: {{USER_GRAMMAR}}

# The second generation entry, selected when the rendered prompt ended INSIDE an
# unclosed thought (entry_state IN_REASONING) because the caller prefilled
# reasoning_content.
#
# The delta then begins in the MIDDLE of `reasoning`, with no `<|channel>thought`
# opener -- that opener is already in the prompt. Parsed with `start` the delta
# has no leading channel block, so the continuation of the thought and the
# `<channel|>` that closes it are read as ordinary content and the reasoning is
# lost.
#
# `reasoning` is reused verbatim, so the same REASONING tag fires and the
# continuation lands in the same field as the part already in the prompt.
resume_reasoning: reasoning channel_close_tag turn_tail
resume_reasoning_tool_call: reasoning channel_close_tag tool_call_request

# The THIRD generation entry: the prompt ended mid-content (entry_state
# IN_CONTENT), so the thought phase is already behind the model.
#
# These are `turn_start`'s four rules minus the leading `channel_block?`, and
# that subtraction is the whole point. Sharing `turn_start` let a turn open a
# thought the ordering says it is past, and the state that reaches here is
# EXACTLY the state where that is wrong:
#
#   * the empty-thought prefill (`enable_thinking: false`), which opened AND
#     CLOSED a thought in the prompt -- so another one is a SECOND thought;
#   * `continue_final_message` resuming mid-answer, where the model is part-way
#     through a sentence;
#   * carrying on in the same turn after a tool response with thinking off.
#
# The first is the one that matters in practice. The empty-thought prefill exists
# to suppress the "ghost thought channel" Gemma 4 emits on a thinking-off
# structured call -- Google's own fix, and without it a call burns ~4050 tokens
# and returns nothing (measured: 10/100 failures on E2B against its own template,
# 0/100 against the 12B-it one). That prefill is prompt-shaping, which is a
# nudge; this makes the same thing STRUCTURAL, so a thinking-off request cannot
# open a thought channel whatever the model would prefer.
resume_content: turn_tail
resume_content_tool_call: tool_call_request
resume_content_response_format: {{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} response_format turn_close_tag?
resume_content_user_grammar: {{TOOL_CALL_ALT}} user_content turn_close_tag?

# --- Assistant turn continuation (sampler entry) -------------------------------
#
# A channel is the tag plus its KIND. `thought` is the only kind the model emits
# today, but it is a production rather than part of the opener, so a different
# kind is a grammar question and not a new literal to remember.
channel_block: channel_open channel_body channel_close_tag
channel_open: channel_open_tag channel_kind
channel_kind: "thought"
channel_body: reasoning
# Both bodies are unrestricted runs of characters, and need no exclusion at all:
# the markers that end them are special tokens, which a regex cannot produce. The
# sampler simply cannot carry a body past the marker, whatever the body contains.
# So a thought or an answer may hold `<`, `<html>`, backticks -- anything.
#
# Everything previously tried here was working around markers being ordinary
# bytes: negative lookahead (llguidance rejects look-around outright), a
# lookahead-free exclusion (its lexer is maximal-munch with no backtracking, so a
# body that could consume even a PREFIX of a marker committed and dead-ended), the
# documented `[lazy]` option (panics: `assertion failed:
# !state.has_lowest_match()`), and a hand-built set of short lexemes for the parser
# to join. All symptom, one cause.
reasoning: /(.|\n)*/
content: /(.|\n)*/

# A turn that CALLS tools does not close with `<turn|>`. It hands over by OPENING
# the response block and stopping there, so the runtime can fill the response in
# and the model carries on INSIDE the same turn -- which is also why a thought
# re-opener, not a fresh `<|turn>model`, follows a tool response.
#
# `<|tool_response>` is therefore this turn's closing token rather than an
# optional extra, and POLICIES.md#closing-tokens-are-required covers it like any
# other. Both normative sources agree, so there is nothing to arbitrate: S3's
# generation grammar makes `fc_resp` mandatory, and the canonical template emits
# it in place of `<turn|>` for an assistant message whose calls have no responses
# yet (S7:369-370).
#
# It was missing here while the renderer already emitted it, so the sampler
# forbade the one token the model had to produce next: a grammar violation at the
# end of every tool-calling turn.
tool_call_request: tool_calls tool_response_open_tag

tool_calls: tool_call+
# Open, directive, colon, payload, close -- the shape shared by the whole
# tool family. `call` is a directive, not part of the tag.
#
# {{TOOL_SCHEMA}} is replaced per request with one alternative per declared tool,
# each pairing that tool's NAME LITERAL with that tool's own argument shape, so a
# call cannot name one tool and carry another's arguments. With no tools declared
# it falls back to `tool_call_directive ":" func_name gemma4_dict`, the
# unconstrained form.
tool_call: tool_call_open_tag {{TOOL_SCHEMA}} tool_call_close_tag
tool_call_directive: "call"
func_name: FUNC_NAME
# The PARSER's charset, which is deliberately wider than the writer's: S1 admits
# `.` and `-` in an identifier, and every MCP tool name uses them
# (`filesystem.read_file`, `github-search`). Generation does not reach this rule
# at all -- {{TOOL_SCHEMA}} names each declared tool as a literal -- so widening
# it cannot loosen the sampler. It only stops extraction from dropping a call
# whose name the model was explicitly told to emit.
FUNC_NAME: /[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.-]*/

gemma4_dict: "{" (gemma4_kv ("," gemma4_kv)*)? "}"
gemma4_kv: gemma4_dict_key ":" gemma4_value
gemma4_dict_key: /[^:{}]+/
gemma4_value: gemma4_string | gemma4_dict | gemma4_array | GEMMA4_NUMBER | GEMMA4_BOOL | "null"
# The string delimiter is the self-delimiting tag form <|NAME|>, with `"` as the
# name -- not a bespoke marker. Note it is symmetric, which is why the format has
# no way to escape it: see ARCHITECTURE.md.
gemma4_string: str_delim_tag gemma4_str_content str_delim_tag

# Unrestricted, like the other text bodies: `<|"|>` is one token (id 52), so a
# string argument cannot run past its own closing delimiter whatever it contains.
# Which also means a tool argument may hold `<` and angle-bracketed markup.
#
# Lowercase rather than a terminal because the decoder finds it by normalized rule
# name when rebuilding JSON (gemma4_to_json); underscores normalize to the same
# `gemma4-str-content` the uppercase spelling produced.
gemma4_str_content: /(.|\n)*/
gemma4_array: "[" (gemma4_value ("," gemma4_value)*)? "]"

GEMMA4_NUMBER: /-?[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?([eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?/
GEMMA4_BOOL: "true" | "false"

response_format: "```json\n" response_content "\n```"
response_content: {{RESPONSE_SCHEMA}}

# --- Full-conversation walker entry --------------------------------------------
#
# Real intra-turn structure, not an opaque catch-all. The walker runs the SAME
# pipeline over a rendered prompt that it runs over generation, so the decoder
# only emits events for constructs the grammar actually names. `turn_body_text`
# swallowing every turn body as text is why the walk could validate framing and
# still recover nothing.
#
# The assistant turn deliberately reuses `think_block`, `content` and
# `tool_calls` from the generation entry above: one description of the model's
# wire shape, used for both directions.

# This rule describes COMPLETE conversations only. A rendered prompt is a PREFIX
# of one -- it stops exactly where generation is about to begin -- so the walk
# parses it in STREAMING mode and a trailing incomplete turn comes back as
# NEED_MORE_INPUT with its partial nodes intact. That is the parse telling us
# where the model is, which is the whole point.
#
# There is deliberately NO production for a half-finished turn or a thought
# without its `<channel|>`. llguidance enforces this same grammar while sampling,
# so the model cannot emit either; writing them down would describe a language
# the format does not have, and would let the sampler stop mid-thought during
# generation -- the exact failure "closing tokens are required" exists to
# prevent. A prompt that ends mid-thought is not the model emitting an unclosed
# thought, it is the caller having prefilled part of one; that is a property of
# where the input stops, and partial parsing is how a parser expresses it.
conversation: closed_turn*

closed_turn: system_turn | user_turn | closed_model_turn

turn_close: turn_close_tag "\n"

# The system turn carries three things, in this order: the think marker (present
# when the model may use its thought channel this turn), the system text, and the
# tool declarations.
#
# Spelled out rather than swallowed. The body used to be an opaque run stopping
# at a hardcoded `<turn|>` -- `/([^<]|<(?!turn\|>))*/` -- which named one tag
# inside a regex, so the tag vocabulary above could change and this would not
# follow. It could only be written that way because the contents were not
# modelled: a generic "no tag" body would have stopped at `<|think|>`. Naming the
# contents is what lets the exclusion be generic.
# The three text bodies below are reached ONLY through `conversation`, which is
# the PEG walker's entry and never the sampler's -- llguidance is only ever given
# `turn_start` or `resume_reasoning`. The PEG maps these to a run bounded by the
# following element's literals, so a plain greedy regex is correct here and the
# text may contain anything, which matters: a user message is arbitrary.
#
# They must NOT carry `[lazy]`. That option panics this version of llguidance
# (`assertion failed: !state.has_lowest_match()`), and while these rules are
# unreachable from a sampling entry today, leaving the construct here would arm a
# trap for whoever first points llguidance at `conversation`.
system_turn: turn_open_tag "system" "\n" system_body turn_close
system_body: think_marker? system_text tool_declaration*
think_marker: think_tag "\n"
system_text: /(.|\n)*/

# A declaration is the tool tag wrapped around its payload -- the same
# open/payload/close shape as a tool call, and unvalidated beyond that for now.
tool_declaration: tool_decl_open_tag decl_text tool_decl_close_tag
decl_text: /(.|\n)*/

user_turn: turn_open_tag "user" "\n" user_text turn_close
user_text: /(.|\n)*/

# A model turn that completed. Three shapes, all of them things the renderer
# actually emits, and every one of them a REQUIRED literal rather than an
# optional closer (see POLICIES.md#closing-tokens-are-required):
#
#   <turn|>\n                          the ordinary case
#   <|tool_response>...<tool_response|>[<turn|>\n]
#                                      calls answered in-turn; the `<turn|>` is
#                                      present only when the turn also carried
#                                      content, since a turn that ends on a tool
#                                      response stays open for the model to
#                                      resume in
#   <|tool_response>                   calls outstanding -- the bare opener, the
#                                      same token generation ends on
#
# The last of those was unrepresentable, so any prompt replaying an assistant
# message whose calls had no results yet failed to validate.
closed_model_turn: turn_open_tag "model" "\n" model_body model_turn_close
model_turn_close: turn_close | tool_response_block+ turn_close? | tool_response_open_tag

# Same shape as the generation entry, so the same decoder tags fire.
model_body: channel_block? content (channel_block content)* tool_calls?

# Same open/directive/colon/payload/close shape as tool_call.
tool_response_block: tool_response_open_tag tool_response_directive ":" func_name gemma4_dict tool_response_close_tag
tool_response_directive: "response"

# NO %ignore. Whitespace is significant in this format: content is returned to
# the caller verbatim, and a global skip silently ate the trailing space of a
# prefilled continuation ("Hello, " came back as "Hello,"). Every literal in
# this grammar spells out the whitespace it expects.
